## Priorities

Nibs can have an optional priority. Use `-p` when creating or `--priority` when updating:
{{range .Priorities}}
- **{{.Name}}**{{if .Description}}: {{.Description}}{{end}}
{{- end}}

Nibs without a priority are treated as `normal` priority for sorting purposes.

## Estimates

Nibs can have an optional estimate using t-shirt sizes. Use `-e` when creating or `--estimate` when updating:

- **s**: Small (1 point)
- **m**: Medium (3 points)
- **l**: Large (5 points)
- **xl**: Extra Large (8 points)

Nibs without an estimate default to Medium (3 points) for weight calculations.

## Body Section Conventions

Each nib type has standard sections. Use these when creating nibs (templates are pre-filled in the TUI):

- **task**: `## Description`, `## Verification` (checklist)
- **bug**: `## Steps to Reproduce`, `## Expected vs Actual`, `## Root Cause`
- **epic**: `## Objective`, `## Acceptance Criteria` (checklist), `## Scope Boundaries`
- **milestone**: `## Goal`, `## Current Focus`, `## Key Decisions`
- **research**: `## Question`, `## Findings`, `## Decision`, `## Follow-ups`
- **feature**: No standard template (features vary too widely)

When **completing** a nib, append a `## Summary of Changes` section. When **scrapping**, append `## Reasons for Scrapping`.

## Reviewing Plan Progress

Use `nibs plan` to review ordered children of a parent nib with their status and acceptance criteria:

```bash
nibs plan --json <parent-id>            # All children with position, status, acceptance criteria
nibs plan --json <parent-id> --active   # Exclude completed/scrapped items
```

This is useful for checking progress on an epic or milestone before closing it.

## Deciding What Order to Tackle Children

Use `nibs links --rel children --order topo` when some children depend on others finishing first. It reads each child's `blocked_by` declarations to build a dependency graph and topologically sorts the subtree:

```bash
nibs links --json <parent-id> --rel children --order topo             # Topologically ordered children
nibs links --json <parent-id> --rel children --order topo --active    # Exclude completed/scrapped before sorting
```

Edges come from `blocked_by` declarations only: if child B has `blocked_by: [a]` (where A is a sibling of B), then A must come before B. `blocked_by` entries pointing to nibs outside the subtree are silently dropped (they don't affect internal order). Self-references in `blocked_by` are ignored. Cycles raise an error naming the participating nibs so you don't silently plan against an unsatisfiable order. **`#<id>` mentions in body prose are informational only and do NOT contribute edges** — to express a real ordering dependency, set the dependee's `blocked_by` field.

## Reordering Nibs

Use `nibs reorder` to change a nib's position among its siblings, or to move multiple siblings at once.

**Single nib** — reposition one nib relative to a sibling or to the front:

```bash
nibs reorder --json <id> --after <sibling-id>
nibs reorder --json <id> --before <sibling-id>
nibs reorder --json <id> --first
```

Equivalent to `nibs update <id> --after/--before/--first`; either form works.

**Mode A: declarative children list** — set the entire order under a parent. Every direct child of `<parent-id>` must be listed exactly once (strict completeness):

```bash
nibs reorder --json --children-of <parent-id> <child1> <child2> <child3>
nibs reorder --json --children-of "" <root1> <root2> <root3>     # root-level siblings
```

Use this when you want to define the order definitively (e.g. after planning out a phase). Omitting or duplicating a child errors before any write.

**Mode B: block move** — move a contiguous run of siblings as a unit. Parent is inferred from the listed nibs (they must share one); the anchor must be a sibling not in the moving block:

```bash
nibs reorder --json <id1> <id2> <id3> --after <anchor>
nibs reorder --json <id1> <id2> <id3> --before <anchor>
nibs reorder --json <id1> <id2> --first
```

Use this when you want to slide a few related nibs to a new position without touching the rest of the order.

## Closing Nibs

Use `nibs close` to finish a phase or plan item. It marks the nib completed, appends a summary, and updates the parent milestone:

```bash
nibs close <id> --summary "What was accomplished"
nibs close <id> --summary "Done" --force    # Close even if children are incomplete
nibs close <id> --summary "Done" --json     # Machine-readable output
```

What `close` does:
1. Checks all children are completed/scrapped (error if not, unless `--force`)
2. Appends a `## Summary` section to the nib's body
3. If the nib has a parent, updates the parent's `## Current Focus` section
4. If the nib has a `## Key Decisions` section, merges those into the parent's `## Key Decisions`
5. Marks the nib as completed

**When to use `close` vs `update -s completed`:** Use `close` when finishing epics or features under a milestone — it handles summary and parent propagation automatically. Use `update -s completed` for simple tasks where no summary or parent update is needed.

## Milestone Management

Milestones use `## Current Focus` and `## Key Decisions` sections to track ongoing progress:

- **Current Focus**: Updated automatically by `nibs close` when a child is closed. Shows the latest completed work.
- **Key Decisions**: Accumulated from child nibs via `nibs close`. Record important decisions in your nib's `## Key Decisions` section and they will be merged into the parent milestone when you close.

Pattern for working under a milestone:
1. Check the milestone's Current Focus and Key Decisions for context
2. Work on a child nib (epic/feature/task)
3. Record any important decisions in the child's `## Key Decisions` section as you go
4. When done, use `nibs close <id> --summary "..."` to close and propagate to the milestone

## Modifying Nib Body Content

Use `nibs update` to modify body content along with metadata changes:

**Replace text (exact match, must occur exactly once):**
```bash
nibs update <id> --body-replace-old "- [ ] Task 1" --body-replace-new "- [x] Task 1"
```
- Errors if text not found or found multiple times
- Use empty string to delete the matched text

**Append content:**
```bash
nibs update <id> --body-append "## Notes\n\nAdded content"
echo "Multi-line content" | nibs update <id> --body-append -
```
- Adds text to end of body with blank line separator
- Use `-` to read from stdin

**Combined with metadata changes:**
```bash
nibs update <id> \
  --body-replace-old "- [ ] Deploy to prod" --body-replace-new "- [x] Deploy to prod" \
  --status completed
```

**Multiple replacements (via GraphQL):**
```bash
nibs query 'mutation {
  updateNib(id: "<id>", input: {
    status: "completed"
    bodyMod: {
      replace: [
        { old: "- [ ] Task 1", new: "- [x] Task 1" }
        { old: "- [ ] Task 2", new: "- [x] Task 2" }
      ]
      append: "## Summary\n\nAll tasks completed!"
    }
  }) { id body etag }
}'
```
- Replacements execute sequentially (each operates on result of previous)
- Append applied after all replacements
- All operations atomic with single etag validation
- Transactional: any failure = no changes saved

## Concurrency Control

Use etags with `--if-match`:
```bash
ETAG=$(nibs show <id> --etag-only)
nibs update <id> --if-match "$ETAG" ...
```

On conflict, returns an error with the current etag.

For bulk reorder operations (`nibs reorder --children-of` and the multi-nib block move), use `--child-if-match <id>=<etag>` (repeatable) to validate multiple etags at once. Pre-validation is atomic: a stale or unknown etag aborts the whole operation before any writes. `--child-if-match` is mutually exclusive with `--if-match`.

## GraphQL Queries

The `nibs query` command allows advanced querying using GraphQL.

- Fetch exactly the fields you need, across a potentially large set of nibs
- Directly read all fields (including `body`) and relationships
- Traverse relationships in a single query
- Execute mutations to create and update nibs
- `nibs query --help` for syntax and usage details
- `nibs query --schema` to view the full GraphQL schema

```bash
# Get all actionable nibs with their details
nibs query --json '{ nibs(filter: { excludeStatus: ["completed", "scrapped"], isBlocked: false }) { id title status type body } }'

# Get a single nib with its relationships
nibs query --json '{ nib(id: "nib-abc") { title body parent { title } children { id title status } } }'

# Find high-priority bugs
nibs query --json '{ nibs(filter: { type: ["bug"], priority: ["critical", "high"] }) { id title } }'

# Search with text
nibs query --json '{ nibs(filter: { search: "authentication" }) { id title body } }'
```
